WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.862
BETWEEN and IN are two readable filtering patterns.

00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:07.462
BETWEEN is helpful for a continuous range.

00:00:07.462 --> 00:00:13.002
When we say age between thirty and sixty, both endpoints are included.

00:00:13.002 --> 00:00:18.097
IN is useful when a column may match one of several listed values.

00:00:18.097 --> 00:00:23.657
Instead of writing several OR conditions, we can list the allowed values together.

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:28.842
This example combines a range condition with a categorical condition.

00:00:28.842 --> 00:00:35.817
Before running it, read the WHERE clause in plain language and predict which records can qualify.

00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:41.102
Then run the query and compare the returned rows with your prediction.

00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:48.387
When a filter combines multiple conditions, check whether AND or OR matches the actual requirement.

00:00:48.387 --> 00:00:56.242
The SQL can be syntactically valid and still answer the wrong question if the logical connector is wrong.

00:00:56.242 --> 00:01:00.327
Think of SELECT work as progressively shaping a question.

00:01:00.327 --> 00:01:08.667
First identify the rows and columns you need, then filter, derive expressions, sort, and limit for inspection.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:14.467
A useful habit is to test each part on a small result before adding more complexity,

00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:17.840
especially when a filter contains NULL-sensitive logic.
